PHASE 1 — RECON
═══════════════
tool:  nmap

The Crazy Ants run on 10.4.12.0/24. The terminal you're on has real
nmap installed, real DNS resolving their hostnames, and a real
network connection into their range. Hit it.

    nmap 10.4.12.0/24       full sweep, all addresses
    nmap -sV 10.4.12.0/24   same, but ask each host its software version
    nmap 10.4.12.88         zoom on a single host
    nmap --help             real nmap help (it's long)

Long output? Pipe through less:    nmap -sV 10.4.12.0/24 | less


WHAT YOU'RE ACTUALLY LOOKING FOR
────────────────────────────────
Recon isn't about finding "vulnerabilities." Anyone can spot an old
website. What recon is really about is finding evidence of human
carelessness. Somebody at this company did something they didn't
think anybody would notice — and left a trace.

Most of what nmap returns will be normal: their public website, the
mail server, the customer-facing API. These are well-locked-down and
supposed to be there. Skip past them.

What you want is the host that exists because somebody made a
decision they didn't clean up after. A test server spun up for a
sprint three years ago and never torn down. A staging environment
that was supposed to be internal but got accidentally exposed when
someone set up a port forward "just for today" and forgot.

If you can find that host — visible from outside but clearly meant
for internal use — that's the back office. That's where the
fragrance blueprint lives.

When you have it, submit the host name or the IP:

    apply m0use{<hostname>}        or       apply m0use{<ip-address>}

Stuck?  cat hint  — no penalty.
